# The v12 DOGFOOD worker image: the reference worker, with a real provider.
#
# W39357. Every line carries the decision behind it, like W6633's recipe does,
# because each one is either inherited from a measured fact or is a deliberate
# departure from the reference image.
#
# THIS IS NOT `Dockerfile` AND NOT `Dockerfile.claude` IN THE SPIKE. W6633's
# reference image runs `ScriptedAgent`, carries no network client and pins its
# base by digest; the spike's image runs `trial.mjs` on its own Docker
# lifecycle. This one runs W6633's OWN PROGRAM -- `baton_worker.py`, unmodified
# -- with a real provider injected at its documented seam, and it speaks the
# accepted worker-entry protocol rather than the spike's result shape.
#
# THE BASE IS W17110's, and that is a reuse of measured installation facts
# rather than of its protocol. A real agent runtime is a Node program, so the
# reference image's `python:3.13-slim` cannot host it.
#
# THIS RECIPE IS NOT REPRODUCIBLE ACROSS DAYS, and W55361 measured it rather
# than predicting it: two builds of an unchanged tree produced
# `sha256:b471399a…` and `sha256:8af96742…`, identical in the base layers,
# in the four `COPY` layers carrying this repository's own code and in
# `useradd`,
# and differing ONLY in the `npm install` and `apt-get install` layers below.
# The three moving inputs are named where they occur: this `FROM` is a TAG
# rather than a digest, `apt-get` takes whatever the mirror serves today, and
# the pinned provider version runs a `postinstall` that fetches its own native
# binary.
#
# SO THE ARTEFACT IS SELECTED, NOT REBUILT. Approver event 55641: the owning
# Work's record selects a validated digest, a new attempt reuses the current
# selection, and a build here is a CANDIDATE until a recorded upgrade, source,
# security, platform or refresh event validates and explicitly selects it.
# Locking these three inputs is a supply-chain design that was presented and
# deliberately NOT selected for the MVP; it remains available and is not
# authorized by this comment.
#
# THE SUITE IS TRIXIE AND THAT IS THE WHOLE W85497 CORRECTION. `v12/python/
# pyproject.toml` declares `requires-python >= 3.13`, and Debian BOOKWORM's
# unversioned `python3` is 3.11 -- so the first ordinary self-hosted W71917
# retry ran a provider that succeeded and a verification that could not, with
# `python3 -m compileall` reporting an unterminated string at
# `worker_manager/custody.py:752` in source no candidate had touched. The
# image was rejecting syntax this distribution's floor makes legal.
#
# WHY THE SUITE AND NOT A SECOND INTERPRETER. Installing `python3.13` beside
# Debian's own, or importing one from `python:3.13-slim` in a second stage,
# both leave TWO Pythons in the artefact and make "which one ran" a question
# the evidence has to answer for every future probe. Moving the suite leaves
# exactly one `python3`, keeps every apt package NAME in this recipe unchanged,
# and keeps Node 22 -- which is the provider runtime and the reason this image
# is not the reference image's `python:3.13-slim`.
#
# MEASURED, NOT INFERRED: `node:22-trixie-slim` plus this recipe's own
# `apt-get install python3` answers `Python 3.13.5` on Debian 13.6. The gate
# in `tests/manager/test_dogfood_image.py` asks the built ARTEFACT rather than
# trusting this comment, because a recipe naming a suite and an image carrying
# an interpreter are two facts and only the second one runs.
FROM node:22-trixie-slim

# THE PROVIDER RUNTIME, BY EXACT VERSION. "Which agent answered" is a fact the
# evidence has to carry, and a floating tag makes that unanswerable the day
# after the trial. This is W17110's pinned version.
ARG CLAUDE_VERSION=2.1.247
RUN npm install -g "@anthropic-ai/claude-code@${CLAUDE_VERSION}" \
    && npm cache clean --force

# THE SYSTEM TRUST STORE, and W17110 paid two review rounds to learn why.
# `node:22-bookworm-slim` ships no `/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt`, and a
# runtime that is a NATIVE BINARY uses the system store rather than Node's
# bundled one -- so every TLS handshake fails and surfaces as a connection
# error that reads exactly like a network fault. The classifier was right and
# was describing a defect in the recipe.
#
# PYTHON COMES WITH IT, because `baton_worker.py` is the program in this image
# and the base is a Node image. The reference image's rule -- no build
# toolchain, no package manager state, no network client -- cannot hold here:
# a provider-backed worker IS a network client, and that is the whole point of
# the milestone. What is kept from the rule is that nothing else is added.
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install --no-install-recommends -y ca-certificates python3 \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# THE WORKER, UNMODIFIED, AND ITS FROZEN CONTRACT AS DATA.
#
# `baton_worker.py` is W6633's file and is copied rather than adapted: this
# checkpoint does not own it, and an image that carried a forked copy would be
# running a protocol nobody reviewed. `worker-control-1.0.schema.json` travels
# for the reason W6633 gives -- the closed member sets are DERIVED from it at
# startup instead of being a hand-typed second copy.
#
# NOTHING FROM `baton_v12` IS COPIED IN, exactly as in the reference image: a
# worker that could import the manager is a worker one bug away from holding
# the manager's capabilities.
COPY baton_worker.py /opt/baton/baton_worker.py
COPY claude_agent.py /opt/baton/claude_agent.py
COPY dogfood_entry.py /opt/baton/dogfood_entry.py
COPY worker-control-1.0.schema.json /opt/baton/worker-control-1.0.schema.json

# `scripted_agent.py` DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TRAVEL, and the reason is history
# worth keeping rather than an absence nobody has to explain.
#
# W39357's build gate found what recipe inspection could not: `main(agent=...)`
# opened with an UNCONDITIONAL `from scripted_agent import ScriptedAgent`, so
# the documented injection seam could not be used by an image that did not also
# ship the default it was overriding, and the entrypoint died
# `ModuleNotFoundError` before the worker started. `COPY scripted_agent.py` was
# the stated stopgap for two rounds.
#
# W39770 is the real correction and is CLOSED SATISFYING: the scripted default
# is constructed lazily by `_scripted_default()` and only for `agent is None`.
# Its rationale assigns this removal to W39357, so the stopgap goes here, and
# `test_dogfood_image` asserts the module's ABSENCE from the artefact — a real
# provider image has no business carrying a scripted provider.

# THE SAME FIXED NON-ROOT USER, by numeric id, as the reference image and as
# the adapter's `--user` restriction. Two places agreeing because they were
# written from one decision.
#
# THE HOME IS CREATED AND OWNED AT BUILD TIME, which W17110 measured the need
# for: Docker creates a missing bind-mount parent as ROOT, so a container
# running as an unprivileged uid was left unable to write beside its own
# credential -- "Permission denied" before it ever reached an authentication
# decision. This image's credential does NOT arrive as a mount there (the
# adapter links it into private `/tmp` space, because the root filesystem is
# read-only), and the owned home is kept anyway so the provider has somewhere
# valid to fall back to for its own state.
RUN useradd --uid 65532 --create-home nonroot \
    && mkdir -p /home/nonroot/.claude \
    && chown -R 65532:65532 /home/nonroot
USER 65532:65532

# The entrypoint is the REFERENCE WORKER's program with this image's agent
# injected -- one line of composition in `dogfood_entry.py`, so the injection
# is a reviewable artefact rather than a shell incantation.
#
# No shell form, so there is no shell in the process tree and nothing to
# interpret a signal or a metacharacter on the worker's behalf. The manager
# stops this container by signalling PID 1, and PID 1 is Python.
ENTRYPOINT ["python3", "/opt/baton/dogfood_entry.py"]

# Deterministic bytes and an explicit module path, as in the reference image.
#
# `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` IS NOT THE CACHE BOUNDARY AND MUST NOT BE READ AS
# ONE. W85497 measured both halves of why. `compileall` writes bytecode as its
# PURPOSE and ignores this variable -- a local 3.13 reproduction produced
# `__pycache__/probe.cpython-313.pyc` with it set -- and `claude_agent`
# composes its children's environments member by member rather than inheriting
# this process's, so an image-level `ENV` reaches neither child in the first
# place. The boundary is `PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX` on the composed environments in
# `claude_agent.py`, and it is written there. This line stays for the
# interpreter's OWN imports inside the container, which is all it ever did.
ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 \
    PYTHONPATH=/opt/baton \
    PYTHONHASHSEED=0 \
    NODE_NO_WARNINGS=1

# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `ENV ANTHROPIC_*` OF ANY KIND, and the reason is
# sharper than "secrets don't belong in images". The provider resolves its
# credential from the environment FIRST: an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` present here
# would silently outrank the mounted slot and decide which account every
# container built from this image ran as. The adapter composes the child's
# environment itself and forwards nothing.
#
# There is also no `ENV BATON_WORKER_*`: that transport was retired with no
# fallback (W26291), and this image's worker does not read those names at all.
#
# No `VOLUME`, no `EXPOSE`, no `HEALTHCHECK`: the writable output is a mount
# the manager names per assignment, and this container's health is observed by
# the adapter through the engine rather than announced by the container.
